always get confused between Collector and Collectors. help me to understand the difference

This confusion is very common 🙂 — the names are unfortunately similar. Let’s untangle them clearly and permanently.

1️⃣ Collector (singular) — the idea / contract

Collector is an interface.

Think of it as:

“A recipe that tells Java how to collect stream elements.”

It defines what is needed to perform a collection:

How to create the container

How to add elements

How to combine partial results (for parallel streams)

How to finish the result

public interface Collector<T, A, R> {
    Supplier<A> supplier();
    BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator();
    BinaryOperator<A> combiner();
    Function<A, R> finisher();
    Set<Characteristics> characteristics();
}


👉 You never call these methods yourself.
The Stream API does that internally.

2️⃣ Collectors (plural) — the factory / toolbox

Collectors is a utility class full of static factory methods that create Collector objects.

Think of it as:

“A toolbox that gives you ready-made Collector recipes.”

Examples:

Collectors.toList()
Collectors.toSet()
Collectors.toMap(...)
Collectors.groupingBy(...)
Collectors.joining()


Each of these returns a Collector.

Collector<T, ?, List<T>> collector = Collectors.toList();

3️⃣ How they work together
collect() expects a Collector
<R, A> R collect(Collector<? super T, A, R> collector)


So you pass it one:

List<String> list = stream.collect(Collectors.toList());


What’s happening:

Collectors.toList() → creates a Collector

collect(...) → uses that Collector

4️⃣ Analogy (easy to remember)
🧑‍🍳 Cooking analogy
Java	Cooking
Collector	A recipe
Collectors	A recipe book
collect()	The cook

You:

stream.collect(Collectors.toList());


Means:

“Hey cook, use the recipe from the recipe book to prepare my dish.”

5️⃣ Custom Collector (rare, but clarifies things)

You can create your own Collector (usually only in advanced cases):

Collector<String, List<String>, List<String>> myCollector =
        Collector.of(
            ArrayList::new,
            List::add,
            (left, right) -> { left.addAll(right); return left; }
        );


And use it:

List<String> list = stream.collect(myCollector);


⚠️ Notice: you did not use Collectors here — because you built the Collector yourself.

6️⃣ One-line memory trick

Collectors create Collectors
collect() consumes a Collector

7️⃣ When to use which?
You want to…	Use
Collect stream data	collect()
Get a ready-made collector	Collectors
Implement custom collection logic	Collector

